  "subtitle": "A machine that reads your whole hand as a password — retro-reflective road-sign optics, a memory chip tricked into being a camera, and a ritual printed on the platen",
  "why_scout_cares": "The ID-3D turned the human hand into a living password. The user types a PIN, then performs a printed choreography on a retro-reflective platen — slide the hand forward until it bumps the web pegs, press flat, close fingers against the pins, hold until the lamp goes out — and the machine builds a three-dimensional silhouette of the hand using a Micron IS-32 'Optic RAM': a dynamic RAM chip in a clear-window package, where photons charge the memory capacitors and the bit pattern becomes the image. Two orthogonal views (plan + side via a 45-degree mirror) defeat the cardboard-tracing attack that fooled 2D readers. First commercial hand-geometry systems appeared in 1986; the line became the HandKey and later admitted people to Disney World turnstiles and the 1996 Atlanta Olympics. The museum has tokens, cards, and codes — but no artifact whose whole point is that your body, measured in three dimensions, is the key. Nothing else in the collection turns the user into the credential.",
  "search_terms": ["Recognition Systems ID-3D", "hand geometry identity verifier 1986", "David Sidlauskas hand geometry", "US 4736203", "HandKey hand geometry", "Optic RAM Micron IS-32"],
